// Title: Process Creation Attempt via Wmic.EXE
// Sigma ID: 526be59f-a573-4eea-b5f7-f0973207634d
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Michael Haag, Florian Roth (Nextron Systems), juju4, oscd.community
// Date: 2019-01-16 | Modified: 2023-02-14
// ATT&CK: execution, T1047
// Tags: car.2016-03-002
// Description: Detects the attempt to create a process via "wmic" with the "process call create" flag, which
//   might indicate an attempt to execute a malicious process on the compromised host. Adversaries
//   may use wmic to execute a process on the compromised host as part of their attack. This event is
//   triggered on on attempt and process creation can be either successful or unsuccessful.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://www.sans.org/blog/wmic-for-incident-response/
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/84215139ee5127f8e3a117e063b604812bd71928/atomics/T1047/T1047.md#atomic-test-5---wmi-execute-local-process
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_wmic_process_creation.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\wmic.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "wmic.exe") and (ProcessCommandLine contains "process" and ProcessCommandLine contains "call" and ProcessCommandLine contains "create")
